IMP72 is the extensible systems language of Ned Irons (IMP stands for Irons
Meta Processor). This is a
compiler for it on 64-bit machines, derived from the IMP72 compiler
Walter Bilofsky wrote for the PDP-10 (DECUS 10-198a, 1973).

The language is defined by a file of syntax statements that the compiler
reads; extensions are written in the same notation. imp compiles
programs to object files, executables, assembly or LLVM IR, and links
executables with the system's ld.lld.

A subroutine is a C function and takes its arguments by reference, as in
FORTRAN. CCALL calls a C function with arguments by value, and an object
file made by imp can be linked with C.

The package also installs impstage0, the bootstrap compiler that builds
imp from source, and the manual.
